{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]],"date-time":"2025-10-29T03:20:37Z","timestamp":1761708037701},"reference-count":9,"publisher":"The Royal Society","issue":"17","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2007,3,28]],"date-time":"2007-03-28T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1175040000000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/royalsociety.org\/journals\/ethics-policies\/data-sharing-mining\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["J. R. Soc. Interface."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2007,12,22]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We argue that the large-dimensional dynamical systems which frequently occur in biological models can sometimes be effectively reduced to much smaller ones. We illustrate this by applying projection operator techniques to a mean-field model of an infectious disease spreading through a population of households. In this way, we are able to accurately approximate the dynamics of the system in terms of a few key quantities greatly reducing the number of equations required. We investigate linear stability in this framework and find a new way of calculating the familiar threshold criterion for household systems.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1098\/rsif.2007.0231","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2007,3,28]],"date-time":"2007-03-28T20:59:57Z","timestamp":1175115597000},"page":"1103-1106","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":12,"title":["Approximate disease dynamics in household-structured populations"],"prefix":"10.1098","volume":"4","author":[{"given":"P.J","family":"Dodd","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology, Imperial College LondonSt Mary's Campus, Norfolk Place, London W2 1PG, UK"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"N.M","family":"Ferguson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Infectious Disease Epidemio